   "reply": "I rewrote `words.py` so `top_words(text, k)` matches the spec. I haven't run it, so the behavior is untested.\n\nThe fix has three parts:\n- **Tokenizing:** a regex `[A-Za-z']+` finds the words, so \"don't\" stays whole and \"well-known\" splits into two words. The old `split()` treated punctuation as part of words.\n- **Case:** words are lowercased before counting.\n- **Ordering:** results sort by count descending, then alphabetically, and the first `k` are returned. The old `most_common` left ties in insertion order.\n\nOne consequence of following the spec literally: a run of only apostrophes, or quotes around a word like `'hello'`, counts as a word with the apostrophes kept.",
